false_positives.candidate:
Team destination calendars silently dropped when organizer has no destinationCalendar

After this PR, `evt.destinationCalendar` can be either a non-empty array (when the event type or the organizer user has a destination calendar) or `null`. The collective branch is:

```ts
if (isTeamEventType && eventType.schedulingType === "COLLECTIVE") {
  evt.destinationCalendar?.push(...teamDestinationCalendars);
}
```

If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `null`, the optional-chained `push` is a no-op. That is exactly the scenario this PR is supposed to support: a COLLECTIVE event whose organizer user has not configured a destination calendar but whose team members have. `teamDestinationCalendars` is collected correctly, but it never reaches `event.destinationCalendar`, so `EventManager.createAllCalendarEvents` falls back to "first connected calendar" and the team hosts are not invited to their calendars. Initialize `destinationCalendar` as `[]` (or fallback to `[]` instead of `null`) and `push` unconditionally.

true_positives.matched_candidate:
enableSqlExpressions has dead code and always returns false

The new `enableSqlExpressions` function is:

```go
func enableSqlExpressions(h *ExpressionQueryReader) bool {
    enabled := !h.features.IsEnabledGlobally(featuremgmt.FlagSqlExpressions)
    if enabled {
        return false
    }
    return false
}
```

Both branches return `false`, making the feature flag check and the `if enabled` branch dead code. The function unconditionally returns `false`, ignoring `featuremgmt.FlagSqlExpressions` entirely. The convoluted logic with `!IsEnabledGlobally(...)` is misleading because it suggests the flag is being respected when it isn't. Either the body should be `return false` with a comment explaining this is a security mitigation, or the function should be written to make the unconditional disablement obvious. As-is, this is hard to reason about and any future maintainer might assume the flag is wired up.

